   .RequireAuthorization(new AuthorizeAttribute { AuthenticationSchemes = "PartnerApi" });
```

Reading an identity in a handler:

```csharp
static async Task<IResult> GetTickets(ClaimsPrincipal user, SupportContext db)
{
    if (user.Identity?.IsAuthenticated != true)
        return Results.Unauthorized();            // belt and braces; RequireAuthorization already did this

    var agentId = long.Parse(user.FindFirstValue(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier)!);
    // ... and note that having agentId is not yet permission to read anything
}
```

That last comment is the bridge to the rest of the chapter. `agentId` tells you who is asking. It does not tell you which tickets are theirs, and nothing in the authentication layer ever will.

