Signing in succeeds and sets the cookie, but every subsequent request to an `[Authorize]` endpoint returns 401 — including for agents who are definitely signed in.

```csharp
var app = builder.Build();

app.UseHttpsRedirection();
app.UseStaticFiles();
app.UseRouting();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.UseAuthentication();

app.MapControllers();
```

What is wrong?

items.bodyMarkdown:
A **policy** is a named authorization rule, declared once and applied by name. That is nearly the whole idea, and the value is entirely in the indirection.

Without it, rules spread:

```csharp
if (user.IsInRole("Supervisor") || user.IsInRole("Admin")) { /* ... */ }
```

repeated in eleven handlers, and when a `TeamLead` role is added, ten of them get updated. The eleventh is the incident.

With a policy the rule has one home:

```csharp
builder.Services.AddAuthorization(options =>
{
    options.AddPolicy("CanReassignTickets", policy =>
        policy.RequireRole("Supervisor", "Admin", "TeamLead"));

    options.AddPolicy("OnSupportTeam", policy =>
        policy.RequireClaim("team", "support"));
});
```

```csharp
app.MapPost("/tickets/{id}/reassign", Reassign)
   .RequireAuthorization("CanReassignTickets");
```

Notice what the endpoint now says. Not 'supervisors and admins and team leads' but **'whoever may reassign tickets'** — the capability, not its current membership. The list changes in one place, and the endpoint keeps telling the truth.

For rules that roles cannot express, write a requirement and a handler:

```csharp
public record MinimumTenure(int Days) : IAuthorizationRequirement;

public class MinimumTenureHandler : AuthorizationHandler<MinimumTenure>
{
    protected override Task HandleRequirementAsync(
        AuthorizationHandlerContext context, MinimumTenure requirement)
    {
        var hired = context.User.FindFirstValue("hired_on");
        if (hired is not null &&
            DateTime.Parse(hired) <= DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays(-requirement.Days))
        {
            context.Succeed(requirement);
        }
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Two details in there are worth pausing on. You call `context.Succeed(requirement)` to allow; you do **not** call `Fail()` to deny. Simply not succeeding is a denial. `Fail()` is stronger — it vetoes the requirement even if another handler would have succeeded it — and reaching for it by mistake produces rules that cannot be satisfied by any combination of handlers.

And now the boundary that the next lesson exists for. Everything above decides using **the identity alone**: roles, claims, tenure. None of it has looked at a ticket. A policy can say 'supervisors may reassign tickets'; it cannot say 'this agent may reassign *this* ticket', because nothing has fetched it.

ASP.NET Core does offer resource-based authorization for exactly that — `IAuthorizationService.AuthorizeAsync(user, resource, requirement)`, called from inside the handler once the resource is loaded. It is the right tool for a genuine multi-actor rule. But for the common case of 'this row belongs to this user', the next lesson argues for something simpler and harder to forget.

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
The same rule, three ways, in increasing order of maintainability.

**Scattered checks** — the rule is wherever someone remembered it:

```csharp
app.MapPost("/tickets/{id}/reassign", async (long id, ClaimsPrincipal user, ...) =>
{
    if (!user.IsInRole("Supervisor") && !user.IsInRole("Admin"))
        return Results.Forbid();
    // ...
});

app.MapPost("/tickets/{id}/escalate", async (long id, ClaimsPrincipal user, ...) =>
{
    if (!user.IsInRole("Supervisor"))          // drifted: Admin silently excluded
        return Results.Forbid();
    // ...
});
```

The second endpoint has already diverged, and nothing will tell you.

**A named policy** — one definition, applied by name:

```csharp
options.AddPolicy("CanReassignTickets", p => p.RequireRole("Supervisor", "Admin"));

app.MapPost("/tickets/{id}/reassign", Reassign).RequireAuthorization("CanReassignTickets");
app.MapPost("/tickets/{id}/escalate", Escalate).RequireAuthorization("CanReassignTickets");
```

Adding `TeamLead` is a one-line change that both endpoints inherit.

**Resource-based, when the rule genuinely needs the row:**

```csharp
static async Task<IResult> Reassign(
    long id, ClaimsPrincipal user,
    IAuthorizationService authorization, SupportContext db)
{
    var ticket = await db.Tickets.FindAsync(id);
    if (ticket is null) return Results.NotFound();

    var result = await authorization.AuthorizeAsync(user, ticket, "CanReassignThisTicket");
    if (!result.Succeeded) return Results.NotFound();

    // ...
}
```

Here the ticket has been loaded, so a handler can compare `ticket.TeamId` against the user's claims — something no endpoint-level policy could do. Note the `NotFound` rather than `Forbid` on failure: for a resource the caller should not be able to see, confirming it exists is itself a disclosure.

