grading.modelAnswerMarkdown:
**What is wrong.** `SupervisorsAndAdmins` names its current membership, not the capability it protects. An endpoint decorated with it says *who may call it today* rather than *why it is restricted*, so the name goes stale the moment the org chart changes — and it gives no hint whether a given endpoint is about ticket handling or billing.

**What that costs now.** Because one policy spans several unrelated capabilities, `TeamLead` cannot simply be added to it: that would grant billing access too. Someone has to open all thirty endpoints, work out which ones are reassignment-and-closure and which are billing, and split them into two policies by hand. That audit is exactly the work the policy indirection was supposed to eliminate, and any endpoint missed in the sweep becomes a silent over-grant.

**How I would restructure it.**

```csharp
options.AddPolicy("CanReassignTickets", p => p.RequireRole("Supervisor", "Admin", "TeamLead"));
options.AddPolicy("CanCloseTickets",    p => p.RequireRole("Supervisor", "Admin", "TeamLead"));
options.AddPolicy("CanChangeBillingSettings", p => p.RequireRole("Admin"));
```

Each endpoint declares the capability it needs. Introducing `TeamLead` becomes a change to two policy definitions and zero endpoints, and reading an endpoint tells you what it is protecting rather than who happened to be trusted when it was written.

items.bodyMarkdown:
This is the lesson the chapter exists for, and you have met the defect before — in V1 chapter 11, and hands-on in mission M03. Here it is in C#:

```csharp
[Authorize]
app.MapGet("/tickets/{id}", async (long id, SupportContext db) =>
{
    var ticket = await db.Tickets.FindAsync(id);
    return ticket is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(ticket);
});
```

Every signed-in agent can read every ticket by guessing an id. The `[Authorize]` is doing real work — it rejects anonymous callers — which is exactly what makes this so easy to ship. The handler looks protected.

The naive fix works and is still wrong:

```csharp
var ticket = await db.Tickets.FindAsync(id);
if (ticket is null) return Results.NotFound();
if (ticket.OwnerId != agentId) return Results.NotFound();   // correct, but fragile
```

Correct for this handler. The trouble is that it is a rule enforced by *remembering* — and there are eleven other handlers. The next one written under time pressure will not have it, and nothing will notice.

The robust version moves ownership into the **query**, so there is no path that returns a row you do not own:

```csharp
var ticket = await db.Tickets
    .FirstOrDefaultAsync(t => t.Id == id && t.OwnerId == agentId);

return ticket is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(ticket);
```

One statement, and the failure mode has changed shape. Forgetting the ownership predicate now means writing a query that does not compile into what you meant, rather than writing a query that silently returns someone else's data. The database never hands you the row in the first place.

The same reasoning extends to updates and deletes — `Where(t => t.Id == id && t.OwnerId == agentId)` before `ExecuteUpdate`, so an unowned row matches nothing — and to lists, where the scope belongs in the base query rather than in a filter applied afterwards.

For a codebase-wide guarantee, EF Core offers **global query filters**:

```csharp
modelBuilder.Entity<Ticket>().HasQueryFilter(t => t.OwnerId == _currentAgentId);
```

Every query against `Tickets` gets the predicate automatically, and forgetting it is no longer possible. That is a strong default with a sharp edge: it applies to *everything*, including admin screens and background jobs, and the escape hatch (`IgnoreQueryFilters()`) is easy to sprinkle around until the guarantee is gone. Worth using deliberately, with the exceptions few and reviewed.

One more decision, and it is a policy rather than a technique: **404 or 403** for a ticket that exists but is not yours? `403` says 'this exists and you may not have it', which confirms the id is real — sometimes an unacceptable disclosure, sometimes fine. `404` says only 'nothing here for you'. Neither is universally right. What is universally wrong is being inconsistent: if `GET` returns 404 and `PATCH` returns 403 for the same unowned ticket, the pair of responses discloses exactly what the 404 was hiding. Pick one, write it down, and make every endpoint follow it — which is precisely what mission M03 asks you to repair.

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
Larkspur's ticket endpoints, before and after.

**Before** — four handlers, four opportunities to forget:

```csharp
app.MapGet("/tickets/{id}", async (long id, SupportContext db) =>
{
    var ticket = await db.Tickets.FindAsync(id);          // no scope
    return ticket is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(ticket);
});

app.MapGet("/tickets", async (SupportContext db, long agentId) =>
    await db.Tickets.Where(t => t.OwnerId == agentId).ToListAsync());   // scoped

app.MapPatch("/tickets/{id}", async (long id, TicketPatch body, SupportContext db, long agentId) =>
{
    var ticket = await db.Tickets.FindAsync(id);
    if (ticket is null) return Results.NotFound();
    if (ticket.OwnerId != agentId)
        return Results.Forbid();                          // scoped, but 403 here and 404 there
    ticket.Subject = body.Subject;
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    return Results.NoContent();
});

app.MapPost("/tickets/{id}/comments", async (long id, CommentBody body, SupportContext db) =>
{
    db.Comments.Add(new Comment { TicketId = id, Body = body.Text });   // no scope at all
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    return Results.Created($"/tickets/{id}/comments", null);
});
```

The list is scoped. The by-id read is not. The patch is scoped but answers `403` where the read answers `404`, so an attacker can distinguish a real id from a fake one by comparing the two responses. The comment endpoint never checks anything and will happily attach a comment to a stranger's ticket. This inconsistency is what real code looks like — one person fixed the list, someone else added the comment route later.

**After** — ownership in the query, one documented policy:

```csharp
// Documented policy: an unowned resource is indistinguishable from a missing one. 404 everywhere.

app.MapGet("/tickets/{id}", async (long id, SupportContext db, long agentId) =>
{
    var ticket = await db.Tickets
        .FirstOrDefaultAsync(t => t.Id == id && t.OwnerId == agentId);
    return ticket is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(ticket);
});

app.MapPatch("/tickets/{id}", async (long id, TicketPatch body, SupportContext db, long agentId) =>
{
    var ticket = await db.Tickets
        .FirstOrDefaultAsync(t => t.Id == id && t.OwnerId == agentId);
    if (ticket is null) return Results.NotFound();

    ticket.Subject = body.Subject;
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    return Results.NoContent();
});

app.MapPost("/tickets/{id}/comments", async (long id, CommentBody body, SupportContext db, long agentId) =>
{
    var owns = await db.Tickets.AnyAsync(t => t.Id == id && t.OwnerId == agentId);
    if (!owns) return Results.NotFound();

    db.Comments.Add(new Comment { TicketId = id, Body = body.Text });
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    return Results.Created($"/tickets/{id}/comments", null);
});
```

Every path that can reach a ticket now names the owner in its predicate, and every unowned resource produces the same response.

